WEBVTT

00:00:02.490 --> 00:00:28.570
Leave it to Beaver, starring Barbara Billingsley, Hugh Beaumont, Tony Dow, Jerry Mathers as the Beaver.

00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:43.250
and two other stores and they didn't have them,

00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:45.490
so I went back to Martinson's and I got the material

00:00:45.490 --> 00:00:47.410
and I just decided I'd make them myself.

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:50.280
Oh, and Beaver was elected co-chairman

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:51.460
of his class cookie committee.

00:00:52.490 --> 00:00:53.430
What happened at the office?

00:00:54.710 --> 00:00:56.090
Well, compared to homemade curtains

00:00:56.090 --> 00:00:57.670
and cookie committees, nothing.

00:00:59.380 --> 00:00:59.980
Thank you.

00:01:01.950 --> 00:01:03.610
Beaver's all excited about the job.

00:01:04.410 --> 00:01:06.850
He's upstairs now counting his money with Wally.

00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:09.880
Counting what money?

00:01:10.490 --> 00:01:13.280
Well, you see, his class is selling cookies at recess.

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:15.540
And then they put the money in their treasury,

00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:18.240
and at the end of the year, they all go on a little trip somewhere.

00:01:19.490 --> 00:01:22.590
Oh, boy, I don't know if I like the idea of Beaver handling a lot of money.

00:01:23.510 --> 00:01:26.110
Well, after all, dear, a boy who's lost three pairs of socks

00:01:26.110 --> 00:01:28.890
on the way home from school is hardly the banker of tomorrow.

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:34.580
Larry Mandello is the other co-chairman, and the way Beaver tells it,

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:38.470
he gets to hold the money one week, and Larry gets to hold it the next.

00:01:39.140 --> 00:01:41.540
Well, at least we only have to worry alternate weeks.

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:47.680
Fifteen, twenty, twenty-five.

00:01:50.130 --> 00:01:53.890
Happy Man brings the cookies at the beginning of the week, and then we sell them.

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:56.920
And then he comes around next week and brings the new cookies,

00:01:57.040 --> 00:01:59.980
and takes the money for the old ones, except for...

00:02:00.190 --> 00:02:01.350
We got left.

00:02:01.630 --> 00:02:03.070
I know, I know, Beaver.

00:02:03.510 --> 00:02:06.190
We had the same thing with chocolate milk when I went to school.

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:09.340
Wally, you're still going to school.

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:12.790
Well, well, I mean when I went to kid's school.

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:16.920
Oh, 20, 25.

00:02:18.130 --> 00:02:19.450
Now, where was I?

00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:21.760
I think you were someplace in the 20s.

00:02:21.860 --> 00:02:24.110
I think I better start over again.

00:02:25.030 --> 00:02:27.520
Well, hi, boys. Hi, Dad.

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:29.980
10, 15...

00:02:32.650 --> 00:02:35.330
I understand you're the head cookie man down at school.

00:02:35.720 --> 00:02:36.340
Yeah, Dad.

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:39.060
Larry and I are the co-chamers of the whole cookie fund.

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:40.100
Yeah, Dad.

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:42.500
He's got $8.35 there.

00:02:42.980 --> 00:02:44.620
Well, that's quite a bit of money.

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:46.860
I just hope you'll be very careful with it.

00:02:47.380 --> 00:02:48.060
Oh, sure, Dad.

00:02:48.450 --> 00:02:50.230
We keep it in this real bank bag,

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:53.440
and we buy the cookies from the factory man,

00:02:54.260 --> 00:02:56.620
and we sign for them with our names, and we make change,

00:02:57.090 --> 00:02:59.930
and Miss Landers says it's all training us for our future...

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:03.820
Hey, Beeb, I thought you wanted to be a lawyer.

00:03:04.260 --> 00:03:06.100
Who ever heard of a lawyer that sells cookies?

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:10.620
Well, a lawyer's gotta do something while he's waiting for people to get in trouble.

00:03:12.040 --> 00:03:15.720
Well, Beeb, I think your cookie fund is very fine training.

00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:19.600
But I do want you to promise me to be careful and conscientious.

00:03:20.210 --> 00:03:22.310
Because, you know, as far as money's concerned,

00:03:22.490 --> 00:03:24.750
this is the first real responsibility you've ever had.

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:28.580
Don't worry, Dad. In the daytime, I'll tie the bag to my belt.

00:03:29.080 --> 00:03:29.980
And in the night...

00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:31.420
sleep in the same bed with it.

00:03:32.700 --> 00:03:34.120
Well, that's fine, Beaver.

00:03:35.690 --> 00:03:39.710
5, 10, 15, 20, 25.

00:03:40.760 --> 00:03:43.920
Hey, Wally, if I did lose the cookie money,

00:03:44.510 --> 00:03:46.410
what do you suppose they'd do to me in school?

00:03:47.380 --> 00:03:50.280
Well, the kids might ostracize you.

00:03:50.520 --> 00:03:51.620
Gee, what's that?

00:03:52.280 --> 00:03:54.300
Well, we just read about it in history.

00:03:54.800 --> 00:03:56.300
It's what they did to Benedict Arnold.

00:03:56.810 --> 00:03:59.970
Oh, did he lose some cookie money?

00:04:03.100 --> 00:04:04.680
He was just one of those traitors.

00:04:04.780 --> 00:04:07.310
But you better watch it anyway.

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:15.000
Mom, how come Beaver left so soon?

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:16.640
He and Larry wanted to get there early.

00:04:17.080 --> 00:04:19.480
Larry said they might sell some cookies before the school opened.

00:04:19.980 --> 00:04:21.100
You better hurry up, too.

00:04:21.640 --> 00:04:22.180
Sure, Mom.

00:04:26.860 --> 00:04:27.240
So long.

00:04:27.730 --> 00:04:28.030
Bye.

00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:34.440
Hey, Mom, can I go to Mexico?

00:04:36.450 --> 00:04:36.850
Mexico?

00:04:37.490 --> 00:04:38.590
Yeah, with Mr. Nelson.

00:04:39.530 --> 00:04:40.610
Then who's Mr. Nelson?

00:04:41.050 --> 00:04:42.190
Well, he's our science teacher.

00:04:42.500 --> 00:04:43.980
He's going to Mexico this summer.

00:04:44.180 --> 00:04:46.520
He said he might take some of the guys from his class with him.

00:04:47.850 --> 00:04:49.260
Well, I don't know, Wally.

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:50.320
What would we have to do?

00:04:50.840 --> 00:04:52.460
All you gotta do is sign this thing,

00:04:52.520 --> 00:04:54.910
saying if we get hurt or killed or anything, it's okay.

00:04:55.630 --> 00:04:59.410
I better ask your father, Wally.

00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:00.980
Bye.

00:05:01.730 --> 00:05:02.050
Bye.

00:05:05.830 --> 00:05:12.940
I want three boxes of raisin cookies.

00:05:13.420 --> 00:05:15.140
And this is a whole dollar.

00:05:17.550 --> 00:05:18.930
Change from a dollar.

00:05:19.350 --> 00:05:20.790
Change for a dollar.

00:05:21.870 --> 00:05:23.670
Change for a dollar.

00:05:24.170 --> 00:05:27.420
One, two, three.

00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:28.860
There you are, Judy.

00:05:30.440 --> 00:05:31.760
You don't got that kind of change.

00:05:32.100 --> 00:05:34.650
You'll either have to give one back or buy one more.

00:05:35.130 --> 00:05:36.950
Well, I'm not gonna buy one more.

00:05:37.910 --> 00:05:38.250
Huh.

00:05:38.670 --> 00:05:41.390
If I'd been elected chairman, I'd have the right change.

00:05:45.020 --> 00:05:45.920
There you are, Judy.

00:05:47.420 --> 00:05:48.340
You're next, Willie.

00:05:49.590 --> 00:05:51.870
You better not eat any of those cookies yourself

00:05:51.870 --> 00:05:53.390
or I'll tell Miss Landers.

00:05:55.070 --> 00:05:56.890
What's the matter with that Judy anyway?

00:05:57.480 --> 00:05:58.420
She's a girl.

00:06:10.360 --> 00:06:11.970
Carry the cookies.

00:06:12.250 --> 00:06:13.110
Yeah, sure, Larry.

00:06:15.240 --> 00:06:17.080
We get to carry the key, too.

00:06:17.520 --> 00:06:19.660
Nobody ever let me carry a key before.

00:06:19.920 --> 00:06:20.880
Yeah, sure, Larry.

00:06:34.860 --> 00:06:51.070
want some help? Yeah, gee, thanks a lot. How are your kids doing? You raking in the money? Oh yeah,

00:06:51.680 --> 00:06:59.660
the count again until recess. Oh here, I'll help you count it now if you want. I better get to class.

00:07:03.940 --> 00:07:07.740
That's Roger DeLacy. He's a new kid in the eighth grade.

00:07:08.290 --> 00:07:09.810
That was okay, him helping us.

00:07:10.320 --> 00:07:14.680
Yeah. Most of the time, the big guys are shoving us around or something.

00:07:15.240 --> 00:07:15.520
Yeah.

00:07:18.950 --> 00:07:22.210
Beaver, what are you all the time jingling the money for?

00:07:22.960 --> 00:07:25.760
That's all right, Larry. Next week, it'll be your turn to jingling.

00:07:30.350 --> 00:07:35.220
Hey, Mom. In here, Wally.

00:07:38.970 --> 00:07:40.290
Hey, Mom, did you ask Dad yet?

00:07:41.420 --> 00:07:41.960
Ask Dad?

00:07:42.450 --> 00:07:43.630
Yeah, about Mr. Nelson.

00:07:44.370 --> 00:07:45.770
Oh, Wally, I'm sorry, I forgot.

00:07:46.570 --> 00:07:47.730
Well, when are you going to ask him?

00:07:48.210 --> 00:07:50.250
Don't worry, Wally, I'll ask him.

00:07:51.200 --> 00:07:53.480
May I ask what it is I'm supposed to be asked?

00:07:53.800 --> 00:07:56.740
Well, I was just wondering if I could go to Mexico this summer.

00:07:57.110 --> 00:07:57.510
Mexico?

00:07:58.330 --> 00:07:59.490
Yeah, with Mr. Nelson.

00:08:00.600 --> 00:08:01.660
Well, who's Mr. Nelson?

00:08:02.660 --> 00:08:03.860
Wally's science teacher, dear.

00:08:05.510 --> 00:08:05.930
Yeah, Dad.

00:08:06.110 --> 00:08:07.850
He's taking a bunch of guys from the class.

00:08:08.300 --> 00:08:11.950
We're all going to go down in station wagons and sleep in sleeping bags,

00:08:11.970 --> 00:08:13.580
and here's all the junk about it.

00:08:14.080 --> 00:08:16.540
Well, I think this is something that'll take a little thought.

00:08:17.890 --> 00:08:19.530
Heck, I thought you'd say no.

00:08:20.370 --> 00:08:21.570
Wally, I didn't say no.

00:08:21.690 --> 00:08:23.270
I said I'd think about it, and I will.

00:08:24.300 --> 00:08:26.600
Wally, did your brother finish his homework?

00:08:27.240 --> 00:08:27.520
Yeah.

00:08:27.760 --> 00:08:29.840
He's counting his cookie money, and then he's going to bed.

00:08:34.540 --> 00:08:36.560
Mexico? What do you think about it?

00:08:37.690 --> 00:08:40.230
I don't know, dear. What do you think about it?

00:08:41.320 --> 00:08:42.720
I think I better think about it.

00:09:07.930 --> 00:09:11.220
If a crook came in after my money, what would you do?

00:09:11.820 --> 00:09:14.920
Well, heck, Beaver, no crooks coming in after your $11.

00:09:16.340 --> 00:09:16.760
No.

00:09:18.220 --> 00:09:19.780
You've seen how it is on television.

00:09:20.420 --> 00:09:24.160
You've got to have at least $2,000 or $3,000 before a crook will ever mess with you.

00:09:25.370 --> 00:09:29.310
Sure hope the crooks in Mayfield watch television.

00:09:34.600 --> 00:09:44.200
It looks like we did pretty good today.

00:09:44.580 --> 00:09:44.940
Yeah.

00:09:45.160 --> 00:09:50.350
What do you think they'll take us on the class trip with the cookie money?

00:09:50.510 --> 00:09:51.460
I don't know.

00:09:51.600 --> 00:09:54.390
I might get to go to Mexico.

00:09:55.110 --> 00:09:56.410
You think we'll get to go to Mexico?

00:09:57.170 --> 00:09:57.530
Nah.

00:09:58.250 --> 00:09:59.750
Nobody ever got to go to Mexico.

00:10:00.030 --> 00:10:00.850
Selling cookies.

00:10:01.570 --> 00:10:02.130
Yeah.

00:10:16.220 --> 00:10:17.580
Oh, you kids want some help again?

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:18.380
Yeah.

00:10:18.830 --> 00:10:19.890
Gee, thanks, Roger.

00:10:29.200 --> 00:10:29.980
What's the matter?

00:10:30.890 --> 00:10:33.010
Nothing. It's just my stomach.

00:10:34.030 --> 00:10:36.330
I guess that comes from not eating for four days.

00:10:37.720 --> 00:10:40.240
You mean you haven't eaten for four whole days?

00:10:41.080 --> 00:10:43.020
Golly, don't you want to eat?

00:10:43.810 --> 00:10:45.650
Gee, everybody wants to eat.

00:10:46.420 --> 00:10:48.600
But how are you gonna eat when there's no food in the house?

00:10:50.660 --> 00:10:55.050
I could get you an apple out of my lunch box.

00:10:55.720 --> 00:10:58.820
No, no, that's all right. I can make it till tomorrow.

00:11:02.240 --> 00:11:05.500
I think we're going to have food then on account of my father gets his paycheck.

00:11:06.480 --> 00:11:07.500
He gets $1,000.

00:11:08.900 --> 00:11:10.520
He always gets that.

00:11:12.820 --> 00:11:15.820
Gee, why didn't he buy food the last time he had $1,000?

00:11:17.720 --> 00:11:19.560
He must have spent it on the way home.

00:11:19.850 --> 00:11:20.730
You know how it is.

00:11:28.690 --> 00:11:29.650
No, no, no.

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:33.210
Well, that's all right. It's my sisters I'm worried about.

00:11:33.810 --> 00:11:35.010
They're both babies.

00:11:36.120 --> 00:11:40.190
Babies? That's the only kind of babies there are.

00:11:41.060 --> 00:11:44.600
If I could find somebody with some money, my father could pay him back tomorrow

00:11:44.600 --> 00:11:46.120
when he gets the thousand dollars.

00:11:46.780 --> 00:11:50.910
But I don't know any nice people with money.

00:11:51.860 --> 00:11:57.490
What does it cost to feed little babies?

00:11:59.040 --> 00:11:59.820
About three dollars.

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:02.400
I don't have to eat anything myself.

00:12:03.200 --> 00:12:19.900
Three dollars? Maybe you could eat with the baby's leaf.

00:12:20.650 --> 00:12:23.510
No, no, no, no. I couldn't take that from you guys, no.

00:12:24.020 --> 00:12:26.840
Oh, it's all right, Roger. You could pay us back tomorrow,

00:12:27.060 --> 00:12:29.080
when your father gets his thousand dollars.

00:12:30.350 --> 00:12:31.950
Well, gee, thanks a lot, kids.

00:12:32.660 --> 00:12:35.160
Oh, you're two of the nicest little kids I ever met.

00:12:37.150 --> 00:12:44.920
You should have given them the cookie money?

00:12:45.560 --> 00:12:48.960
Well, gee, Beaver, you can't let little babies starve.

00:12:49.720 --> 00:12:53.060
And anyway, tomorrow when his father gets $1,000,

00:12:54.120 --> 00:12:57.560
maybe Roger could get him to buy a couple of boxes of cookies from us.

00:12:58.340 --> 00:12:58.820
Yeah.

00:13:00.220 --> 00:13:00.880
We could ask him.

00:13:13.200 --> 00:13:15.730
Beaver, you mean you and that goofy Larry

00:13:15.730 --> 00:13:19.090
just took $3 and handed it over to some kid in school?

00:13:19.520 --> 00:13:21.940
Well, he was starving and holding on to the wall and everything.

00:13:22.610 --> 00:13:24.830
Anyway, he's going to give it back to us tomorrow

00:13:24.830 --> 00:13:26.770
when his father gets a $1,000 paycheck.

00:13:27.690 --> 00:13:29.980
Boy, Beaver, sounds creepy.

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:32.460
I don't think this guy's on the level.

00:13:32.990 --> 00:13:34.790
What do you want to go and say something like that for?

00:13:35.110 --> 00:13:36.970
Well, because that's what I think, that's why.

00:13:37.980 --> 00:13:40.120
You shouldn't say stuff like that to a guy at night.

00:13:40.480 --> 00:13:42.300
He's liable to have all kinds of bad dreams.

00:13:42.840 --> 00:13:44.680
Hey, Bieber, just forget I said it.

00:13:45.270 --> 00:13:47.130
All right, I'll forget you said it.

00:13:55.060 --> 00:13:56.000
Well, what's wrong now?

00:13:56.780 --> 00:13:57.960
I can't forget it.

00:14:01.240 --> 00:14:04.560
Tomorrow morning, go to this Roger guy and get your money back.

00:14:05.870 --> 00:14:06.570
Yeah, sure.

00:14:16.170 --> 00:14:18.140
What if he doesn't have it?

00:14:19.440 --> 00:14:21.720
Well, then you're in a big mess, that's why.

00:14:22.320 --> 00:14:25.570
You know something, Larry? You're a real mean brother.

00:14:33.480 --> 00:14:34.120
Who thought you left?

00:14:36.360 --> 00:14:36.980
Who are you looking for?

00:14:37.300 --> 00:14:38.240
A paperclip.

00:14:38.840 --> 00:14:40.500
Oh dear, they're writing that drawer in that box.

00:14:40.760 --> 00:14:40.980
Oh.

00:14:46.500 --> 00:14:49.200
Now who in the world would do a thing like that?

00:14:49.530 --> 00:14:49.930
Wally.

00:14:50.150 --> 00:14:53.090
He sits there stringing them together when he's talking on the phone.

00:14:53.960 --> 00:14:56.440
A trip to Mexico might be just a thing for that boy.

00:14:57.240 --> 00:14:59.400
Oh, I'll give Beaver a lift if he's ready.

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:02.100
Oh, he and his money bags left a half an hour ago.

00:15:02.360 --> 00:15:03.120
Want more cookies?

00:15:03.400 --> 00:15:03.740
Yes.

00:15:04.740 --> 00:15:06.880
This morning he looked kind of worried about something.

00:15:07.100 --> 00:15:08.760
Well, if you've got to learn about the business world,

00:15:08.860 --> 00:15:09.980
you might as well learn to worry.

00:15:11.800 --> 00:15:13.780
Here he comes now.

00:15:15.340 --> 00:15:15.600
Roger.

00:15:16.180 --> 00:15:16.840
Hiya, squirts.

00:15:19.360 --> 00:15:20.720
We've been waiting for you.

00:15:21.220 --> 00:15:22.140
Yeah? What for?

00:15:22.540 --> 00:15:23.100
For $3.

00:15:23.980 --> 00:15:24.640
What $3?

00:15:25.640 --> 00:15:27.740
The $3 we gave you yesterday.

00:15:28.150 --> 00:15:29.970
Says your sisters that our babies could eat.

00:15:30.570 --> 00:15:32.970
Yeah, it was today.

00:15:33.330 --> 00:15:34.910
When did your father get a $1,000 paycheck?

00:15:36.200 --> 00:15:37.660
What are you guys talking about?

00:15:38.270 --> 00:15:39.430
I don't have any sisters.

00:15:42.660 --> 00:15:45.380
And you didn't give me any $3.

00:15:47.170 --> 00:15:48.450
Sure we did, Roger.

00:15:49.150 --> 00:15:51.410
Don't you remember yesterday by the closet?

00:15:51.530 --> 00:15:53.690
We were almost fainting from not eating.

00:15:54.500 --> 00:15:56.780
Yeah, we gave it to the other of our cookie fund.

00:15:56.940 --> 00:15:57.180
$3.

00:15:58.130 --> 00:15:59.980
Now look, if you guys lost $3.

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:02.760
Three dollars out of your cookie fund. Don't try and put the blame on me.

00:16:03.320 --> 00:16:06.200
But we didn't lose it, Roger. We gave it to you.

00:16:06.790 --> 00:16:10.080
Look, if you guys go around spreading lies like that about me,

00:16:10.500 --> 00:16:13.480
I got a gang, and they're gonna fix you real good.

00:16:14.280 --> 00:16:14.720
Roger!

00:16:15.440 --> 00:16:16.580
Beat it, squirts.

00:16:24.080 --> 00:16:27.380
Yesterday, we gave him three dollars, didn't we?

00:16:27.780 --> 00:16:28.780
Sure we did!

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:33.730
You know something, Larry? He's a crook.

00:16:34.130 --> 00:16:34.530
Yeah.

00:16:35.840 --> 00:16:37.940
Why would we give a crook three dollars?

00:16:44.700 --> 00:16:45.860
Good morning, Miss Landers.

00:16:45.960 --> 00:16:47.920
Good morning. Mr. Preston is here this morning.

00:16:48.470 --> 00:16:49.430
Mr. Preston?

00:16:49.910 --> 00:16:51.070
From the cracker company.

00:16:52.840 --> 00:16:54.480
He's waiting in the principal's office.

00:16:55.090 --> 00:16:56.330
He wants to see us?

00:16:57.490 --> 00:16:59.970
Why, he wants you to turn in your money and order your cookies for...

00:17:02.130 --> 00:17:02.930
I'm slanders.

00:17:02.990 --> 00:17:03.870
Hey, you run along, boys.

00:17:03.990 --> 00:17:05.410
I don't want you to miss any schoolwork.

00:17:05.640 --> 00:17:06.120
Go ahead.

00:17:06.730 --> 00:17:13.100
Boys, I've been over these figures twice.

00:17:13.380 --> 00:17:14.980
I'm afraid your accounts don't balance.

00:17:15.320 --> 00:17:18.320
As a matter of fact, you're $2.95 short.

00:17:19.180 --> 00:17:20.220
$2.95?

00:17:21.120 --> 00:17:21.380
Yeah.

00:17:23.160 --> 00:17:25.300
$2.95 better off than we thought we were.

00:17:27.580 --> 00:17:28.960
You knew you were short.

00:17:31.070 --> 00:17:34.560
Well, what happened? Did you make a mistake and give away too much change?

00:17:37.100 --> 00:17:37.680
No, sir.

00:17:39.010 --> 00:17:39.290
Lose it?

00:17:42.230 --> 00:17:42.890
No, sir.

00:17:43.930 --> 00:17:46.090
Well, then, what happened to the money?

00:17:46.700 --> 00:17:48.680
We gave it to a real big kid.

00:17:50.230 --> 00:17:51.450
You gave it to a big kid?

00:17:52.250 --> 00:17:54.010
Boys, this wasn't your money to give away.

00:17:54.620 --> 00:17:59.960
We know, but this kid was fainting from not eating and holding on to his stuff.

00:18:02.690 --> 00:18:08.070
Yeah, and he said he had two little babies at home that were sisters, and they were starving.

00:18:08.610 --> 00:18:15.450
So we gave him the money, and he said he'd pay us back today when his father got a thousand dollars.

00:18:17.300 --> 00:18:19.820
We even said he could eat what was left over.

00:18:22.000 --> 00:18:25.600
Well, why don't you go to this boy and ask him to give you the three dollars back?

00:18:26.220 --> 00:18:29.980
Well, we didn't. He said he doesn't remember having any sisters.

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:33.040
and he doesn't remember us giving him $3.

00:18:33.340 --> 00:18:35.900
He doesn't even remember being hungry.

00:18:37.100 --> 00:18:38.700
What was this boy's name?

00:18:39.840 --> 00:18:42.080
Oh, oh, we can't tell you that,

00:18:42.440 --> 00:18:44.720
because he's got a whole big gang that'll fix us.

00:18:48.340 --> 00:18:55.190
You know, boys, I've been going around to schools like this for years.

00:18:56.210 --> 00:18:57.350
I've heard some great stories.

00:18:57.920 --> 00:18:59.970
Never one like this.

00:19:01.530 --> 00:19:04.140
The truth, mister, even the two of us together

00:19:04.140 --> 00:19:06.060
couldn't make up a story like this one.

00:19:07.890 --> 00:19:11.060
Well, is a cookie company going to put us in jail?

00:19:11.860 --> 00:19:13.900
Could you please put us under different names

00:19:13.900 --> 00:19:15.260
so our parents won't know?

00:19:15.920 --> 00:19:17.540
Well, you're all right with our company.

00:19:18.340 --> 00:19:20.560
But when you turn your profits over to your class,

00:19:20.580 --> 00:19:22.080
you're going to be $3 short.

00:19:23.510 --> 00:19:24.290
$2.95.

00:19:26.600 --> 00:19:28.420
Are you going to tell Miss Landers on us?

00:19:28.930 --> 00:19:29.150
No.

00:19:32.360 --> 00:19:34.000
No, I'll give you a chance to replace the money.

00:19:34.690 --> 00:19:36.750
I'll give you till tomorrow when I deliver the new order.

00:19:37.350 --> 00:19:38.090
Thank you, mister.

00:19:38.490 --> 00:19:39.850
We'll get it from someplace.

00:19:41.380 --> 00:19:43.620
You know, boys, this is quite a serious thing.

00:19:44.540 --> 00:19:46.520
Your class had confidence enough in you

00:19:46.520 --> 00:19:47.920
to put you in charge of their money.

00:19:48.590 --> 00:19:50.030
We have to trust people in life.

00:19:50.880 --> 00:19:53.220
It's a terrible disappointment when they let you down.

00:19:54.040 --> 00:19:55.020
We know, mister.

00:19:55.660 --> 00:19:58.400
We just found that out with the kid we gave the $3 to.

00:20:04.600 --> 00:20:04.880
Hi, Mom.

00:20:07.760 --> 00:20:08.100
Together.

00:20:08.980 --> 00:20:12.380
Yeah, I found Beaver sitting on a curb over on Grand Avenue,

00:20:12.520 --> 00:20:13.400
so I brought him home.

00:20:13.720 --> 00:20:14.520
Is Dad home yet?

00:20:14.640 --> 00:20:15.380
I saw the car.

00:20:15.540 --> 00:20:16.520
He's in the living room.

00:20:16.710 --> 00:20:18.430
Yeah, I want to talk to him about something.

00:20:21.020 --> 00:20:24.230
Beaver, what were you doing sitting on a curb?

00:20:24.970 --> 00:20:26.490
I wasn't doing anything bad, Mom.

00:20:27.020 --> 00:20:27.940
I was just thinking.

00:20:29.140 --> 00:20:29.420
Oh.

00:20:30.510 --> 00:20:31.450
Oh, hello, Wally.

00:20:32.340 --> 00:20:34.240
Hey, Dad, about the Mexican trip.

00:20:35.420 --> 00:20:37.860
Oh, yeah, I've been thinking that over, Wally,

00:20:37.980 --> 00:20:39.560
and I still want to talk to Mr. Nelson,

00:20:39.740 --> 00:20:41.080
but I don't see any reason why he can't go.

00:20:41.340 --> 00:20:43.940
I'm sure he'll have everything well organized for you guys.

00:20:44.370 --> 00:20:45.810
Yeah, well, that's what I was going to say, Dad.

00:20:45.950 --> 00:20:46.690
It's all off.

00:20:47.310 --> 00:20:48.050
The trip's off?

00:20:48.630 --> 00:20:50.630
Yeah, a couple of guys were horsing around in science

00:20:50.630 --> 00:20:52.690
class today, and Mr. Nelson blew his top.

00:20:53.260 --> 00:20:56.080
Said he wouldn't take anybody in our class to a dogfight.

00:20:56.600 --> 00:20:59.260
That's too bad.

00:21:07.000 --> 00:21:08.220
Hey, would you goof up now?

00:21:09.780 --> 00:21:17.400
You want something, Beaver?

00:21:17.860 --> 00:21:18.600
Yeah, Dad.

00:21:19.830 --> 00:21:21.130
Could I ask you a question?

00:21:21.480 --> 00:21:22.620
Sure. What is it?

00:21:24.000 --> 00:21:29.980
Well, uh, it's a question I'd like you to be listening to when I ask.

00:21:30.440 --> 00:21:34.590
Oh, all right, Beaver. What's the question?

00:21:36.330 --> 00:21:40.790
Well, if I ask it, will you just tell me yes or no without asking why?

00:21:41.540 --> 00:21:42.440
I guess so.

00:21:43.350 --> 00:21:44.490
Can I have a dollar and a half?

00:21:44.890 --> 00:21:46.310
A dollar and a half? What for?

00:21:46.710 --> 00:21:48.330
You said you wouldn't ask me why.

00:21:49.140 --> 00:21:54.490
Oh, yeah, that's right. Well, is this very important to you, Beaver?

00:21:54.730 --> 00:21:55.950
Real important, Dad.

00:21:58.680 --> 00:21:59.000
All right.

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:03.220
I guess your past record is good for a dollar and a half.

00:22:07.600 --> 00:22:08.400
Thanks a lot, Dad.

00:22:09.480 --> 00:22:14.300
Beave, I'm not going to ask why you want this money,

00:22:14.360 --> 00:22:16.040
but you can tell me if you want to.

00:22:17.200 --> 00:22:20.420
If it's a bad reason, you won't take the money back, will you?

00:22:21.550 --> 00:22:21.870
Nope.

00:22:22.370 --> 00:22:25.700
Well, Larry and I were $3 short in our cookie fund.

00:22:26.390 --> 00:22:29.980
Oh, well, whose fault was it, yours or Larry's?

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:33.170
It was on account that we trusted somebody, a kid in school.

00:22:38.080 --> 00:22:38.680
Hi, Beaver.

00:22:48.100 --> 00:22:51.520
Dad, thanks a lot for not asking me a lot of questions.

00:22:52.600 --> 00:22:54.900
Well, Beaver, you may have made a mistake in trusting someone,

00:22:55.100 --> 00:22:57.520
but I'm not going to make the mistake of not trusting you.

00:22:59.080 --> 00:22:59.980
You understand that, son?

00:23:01.950 --> 00:23:06.360
Well, not all of it, but it sure doesn't make me feel good anyway.

00:23:07.270 --> 00:23:10.630
We'll be ready in a half an hour.

00:23:11.340 --> 00:23:11.900
Thanks a lot, Mom.

00:23:20.820 --> 00:23:20.960
Four.

00:23:22.660 --> 00:23:25.360
Well, the way he feels, he had to hug someone,

00:23:25.380 --> 00:23:29.340
and I guess he feels he's getting a little too big to hug his father.

00:23:35.500 --> 00:23:38.120
So you see, Miss Landers, that's how it all happened.

00:23:38.910 --> 00:23:40.410
Oh, I see. I see.

00:23:40.450 --> 00:23:43.370
Miss Landers, Whitey's throwing erasers at Charles.

00:23:43.660 --> 00:23:45.360
You tell them I'll be right in, Judy.

00:23:45.780 --> 00:23:46.960
She'll be right in.

00:23:47.660 --> 00:23:50.480
I want to thank you boys for telling me what happened.

00:23:50.940 --> 00:23:53.540
We're sorry we believed Roger's goofy story, Miss Landers.

00:23:53.830 --> 00:23:57.300
Yeah. We didn't think anybody make it up that he had sisters.

00:23:57.740 --> 00:23:59.980
I'm going to speak to Mrs. Rayburn about Roger.

00:24:00.420 --> 00:24:02.520
and you can be sure he'll be punished.

00:24:03.120 --> 00:24:04.520
All the money's been put back.

00:24:05.200 --> 00:24:08.080
My dad gave me a dollar and a half without asking a single question.

00:24:08.240 --> 00:24:09.680
My Pop gave it to me, too.

00:24:10.600 --> 00:24:13.530
He hollered at me first, but he gave it to me.

00:24:14.270 --> 00:24:16.290
Well, I'm glad this is all straightened out.

00:24:16.840 --> 00:24:20.440
Miss Landers, we don't think we should be the chairman of the cookies anymore.

00:24:20.870 --> 00:24:24.200
Yeah, you should have a chairman that wouldn't even give money away

00:24:24.200 --> 00:24:25.840
to a hundred starving babies.

00:24:26.620 --> 00:24:28.720
Boys, you've done such a good job otherwise.

00:24:29.360 --> 00:24:29.980
Who would I...

00:24:30.000 --> 00:24:31.080
I get to sell the cookies.

00:24:31.520 --> 00:24:33.380
We'd like to nominate Judy Hensler.

00:24:34.240 --> 00:24:34.800
Why Judy?

00:24:35.260 --> 00:24:38.660
Well, if anyone's going to get in trouble with the cookie money,

00:24:38.980 --> 00:24:41.600
we figure Judy's the best one in the class for it.

00:24:42.600 --> 00:24:43.420
No, boys.

00:24:43.940 --> 00:24:46.020
I think you'd better stay on as chairman.

00:24:46.620 --> 00:24:48.380
You know, you've learned by your mistake.

00:24:48.720 --> 00:24:53.710
And, well, I don't know that we could trust even Judy

00:24:53.710 --> 00:24:55.770
not to believe a story like Roger's.

00:24:57.190 --> 00:24:58.170
Now you come on.

00:25:03.360 --> 00:25:03.480
Hey!

00:25:31.720 --> 00:25:32.100
Thank you.
